ling whitespace in Makefile won't be caught by format-check. Not a functional bug.\n\nBut wait — there IS a potential real issue. Let me look at the new rules in the actual Makefile for the exact recipe lines. The diff showed lines like:\n\n```\n+$(TESTDIR)/t3_cpp_too_much_args_case.ok: $(T3_CPP_EXE) $(RUN_CASE) $(CHECK) tests/input_data/t3_too_much_args_case tests/expect/t3_too_much_args_case | $(TESTDIR)\n+\t$(SET_DLL_PATH) && \\\n+        $(RUN_CASE_CMD) \"$(call WCMD,$(T3_CPP_EXE) < tests/input_data/t3_too_much_args_case > $(NULLDEV) 2> $(BINDIR)/t3_bbox_cpp/t3_too_much_args_case)\" $(DATA) && \\\n+        $(CHECK_CMD) $(CONTAINS) $(call WCMD,$(BINDIR)/t3_bbox_cpp/t3_too_much_args_case tests/expect/t3_too_much_args_case) && $(TOUCH) $@ \n```\n\nThese recipes use `$(SET_DLL_PATH) && \\` then a continuation. This is consistent with existing rules. The `\\` continuation in Windows cmd... Actually wait, how does this work? These recipes are for GNU make running cmd.exe. The recipe has `\\` at end of line for make line continuation. The commands are joined. Existing tests work, so the pattern is established.\n\n3. One thing to verify: are there any new .ok rules missing or extra .ok in TESTS lists that don't have rules? `make -n test` passed without \"No rule to make target\" — that means every .ok file has a rule. Good.\n\n4. Let me double-check the specific concern: the new t4 test rules. In the diff:\n```\n$(TESTDIR)/t4_cpp_too_much_coords.ok: $(T4_CPP_EXE) $(RUN_CASE) $(CHECK) tests/expect/t4_too_much_coords tests/input_data/t4_too_much_coords | $(TESTDIR)\n```\nprerequisites include both input and expect files. Good. But note for t4 the order is expect before input (matches existing t4 style). For t3 new rules the order is input then expect (matches existing t3 style). Fine.\n\n5. Now — the `set` file in repo root. It's an untracked 0-byte file. This is junk — likely created accidentally (e.g., `set \"PATH=...\"` redirected?). Actually `set` as a command in cmd doesn't redirect to a file. It might have been created by `set PATH=... > set`? Regardless, it's a stray empty file that should be removed and NOT committed. Worth flagging.\n\n6. Let me also verify there's no stray `.ok` file issue and check the git diff doesn't show other problems. Also I should check whether the new tests are referenced with correct expected exit codes. Let me look at the actual t3/t4 exit-code logic:\n- t3_too_much_args_case → DATA (65) — the program returns exit_code::data for parse errors. Wait, let me check: in t3 main.cpp, on parse error it `return exit_code::data;` — yes 65. And the recipe uses `$(DATA)` = 65. Good.\n- t3_extra_nan → parse_too_much → DATA. Uses $(DATA). Good.\n- t3_x_y_z_4k → parse_not_number → DATA. Good.\n- t3_inf_coord → parse_not_finite → DATA. Good.\n- t3_x_ky_z / t3_x_yk_z → parse_not_number → DATA. Good.\n- t3_scientific → valid input → exit 0. Recipe uses `)\" 0`. Good.\n- t4_* coords errors → DATA. Good.\n- t4_scientific → exit 0. Good.\n\n7. Let me verify the C versions return the same messages exactly. I verified static fragments are present in C sources. And the C messages use same templates (\"Строка %d. Нечисловые данные: %s\\n\" etc). Good — byte-identical after formatting (d=1, s=input line).\n\nBut wait — there's a subtle thing for the C version of parse_point with \"1 2 3 4k\": does it return PARSE_NOT_NUMBER? Let me trace: strtod(\"4k\") → parses \"4\", end points at 'k'. Check: `*end != '\\0' && strchr(\" \\t\\n\\r\", 'k') == NULL` → true → PARSE_NOT_NUMBER. Yes. And \"1 2 3 k4\": strtod(\"k4\") → no digits, end==pointer → PARSE_NOT_NUMBER. And \"1 2 3 4\": strtod(\"4\") → parses, end at '\\0', not in whitespace check passes (since *end=='\\0') → falls to else → PARSE_EXTRA → too_much message. Good. And \"1 2 3 nan\": strtod(\"nan\") parses nan fine (strtod handles nan), end at '\\0', check passes, → PARSE_EXTRA. So extra_nan → too_much message. Consistent.\n\nAnd inf: strtod(\"inf\") parses inf → isfinite fails → PARSE_NOT_FINITE. But note: in C parse_point, the isfinite check happens inside the while loop BEFORE the not-number check, so \"inf 0 0\" → PARSE_NOT_FINITE. Good. But careful: what about \"inf\" in position after XYZ like \"1 2 3 inf\"? In the extra-check branch, the code calls `(void)strtod(pointer, &end)` but does NOT check isfinite — it only checks end==pointer or non-whitespace → so \"1 2 3 inf\" → parsed as number, → PARSE_EXTRA (too_much). In C++ it's the same: from_chars parses inf, → too_much. Consistent between C and C++.\n\n8. Now, one more: the t4 C program — does it read all points and error out with 65 without printing partial? From journal 2026-08-28, t4 C was changed to store like t3. The test uses `> $(NULLDEV) 2> file` and expects $(DATA) code. Regardless of stdout, the exit code and stderr are checked. Fine.\n\nNow the summary of findings. Let me also double check that the input file t3_scientific has no trailing newline and that's fine — the program uses getline which handles both. Reading via text mode: getline reads \"1.5 -2 3e2\" then EOF. Fine.\n\nAlso should verify: the scientific input `1.5 -2 3e2` — C++ from_chars: does it accept \"3e2\"? from_chars for double with chars_format::general default accepts \"3e2\" → 300. Yes. And \"1.5\" and \"-2\". Good.\n\nWait — one concern: `std::from_chars` in MSVC with default format (chars_format::general) accepts scientific notation. Yes.\n\nAnd in C strtod(\"1.5\") etc. Fine.\n\nLet me also check t4_scientific input: \"3e1 1 0.1\\n2e3 4e5 -12\". Wait, \"4e5\" = 400000. Point (2000, 400000, -12). Radius 100. dist huge → filtered. First point (30, 1, 0.1) dist ≈ sqrt(900+1+0.01)=sqrt(901.01)≈30.0167 < 100 → kept. Output \"30.000 1.000 0.100\". Matches expect. \n\nNow let me verify one thing in the Makefile: the t4 rules use `$(T4_CPP_EXE) 1` for the coordinate-error tests — radius 1. And inf test t4_inf_coord uses radius 1 and input \"inf 0 0\". The program checks radius first (valid), then reads points → parse_not_finite → data error. Good.\n\nI think the Makefile and data are consistent. Let me now also confirm the `set` file and present the findings. Also let me double-check that there aren't duplicate/overlapping .ok targets (e.g., both `t3_cpp_scientific.ok` defined twice). Let me grep.",
