_c_symbol_coords test — that's a coordinate-error test (data error → 65). The program returns 65. LSan runs at exit, finds leak, calls _exit(23). So run_case should see 23, not 1.\n\nUnless... the exit code 1 came from something else. Let me reconsider. Actually, maybe the user's actual observed exit code was different. The user said \"ожидаемый код: 65, полученный: 1\". \n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the whole thing. Maybe the leak in t4 was reported but the exit code story is separate. The important question now is: why did t3's leak NOT trigger LSan, but t4's did?\n\nActually, let me re-read the user's question carefully:\n\n\"хорошо. а почему под лиуксом 3я с ошибкой собиралась и лик санитайзер молчал?\"\n\nTranslation: \"ok. and why under Linux did task 3 with the error build/run and leak sanitizer stay silent?\"\n\nAnd earlier: \"1. все тесты под линуксом проходят даже с утечкой в 3 ей задаче\"\n\nSo: all tests under Linux pass even with the leak in task 3. LSan stayed silent for t3 but caught t4.\n\nWait — but t3's leak is `points` allocated at line 16, leaked on the coordinate-error path. t4's leak is `points` allocated at line 129, leaked on the coordinate-error path. Structurally identical. So why would LSan catch t4 but not t3?\n\nHmm. Let me think about the actual difference.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. In t4, the leak was in TWO places potentially. Let me look at the t4 history again. The t4 error in run_case was \"expected 65, got 1\". \n\nActually wait — maybe the exit code 1 is the clue. Let me reconsider what \"1\" means. \n\nIn run_case on Linux: `real_code = WEXITSTATUS(status)`. \n\nIf the process was killed by a signal (e.g., ASan abort due to something), WEXITSTATUS would give the low 8 bits of the status... Actually if the process dies from a signal, WIFEXITED is false and WEXITSTATUS is undefined/garbage (often 0). So 1 isn't from a signal.\n\nActually — maybe exit code 1 was NOT from LSan at all! Let me reconsider. Maybe the t4 program was crashing or aborting for a different reason, returning 1.\n\nHmm, but the user said \"на линуксе ругается что memory leak из объекта созданного на строке 128 - points\". So LSan DID report a leak in t4, from line 128 (the malloc in main). \n\nOK so for t4: LSan reported leak → and run_case saw exit code 1 (not 23, not 65).\n\nFor t3: LSan stayed silent → tests passed → run_case saw 65.\n\nSo the question is specifically: why did LSan report t4's leak but not t3's, when both leak `points` on the error path?\n\nLet me look very carefully. The key difference must be in the code structure.\n\nt4 main.c error path (before fix): \n```c\nint status = get_points(&points, &points_size, &points_capacity);\nif (status != 0)\n    return status;\n```\nAnd get_points returns `data` (65) on coordinate error, without freeing *points. So points leaked. main returns status (65). At exit, LSan sees points leaked.\n\nt3 main.c error path (before fix):\n```c\nif (ex_code != 0) {\n    ...\n    free(s);\n    s = NULL;\n    return data;\n}\n```\npoints (malloc at line 16) leaked. main returns data (65). At exit, LSan should see points leaked.\n\nStructurally identical leaks! Both should be caught. But t3 wasn't.\n\nWait — unless the difference is in LSan's \"still reachable\" vs \"definitely lost\" classification. LSan classifies leaks into:\n- definitely lost: no pointer to the block exists\n- indirectly lost\n- possibly lost (pointer exists but points into the middle of the block, or block is reachable only through a leaked block)\n- still reachable: pointer exists in a global, on stack, or in register\n\nBy default LSan reports all, but \"still reachable\" blocks are NOT counted as leaks by default (actually, LSan's default behavior: \"still reachable\" blocks are reported in the summary but do NOT cause the process to exit with error... actually no).\n\nHmm, actually the key: LSan scans the stack, registers, globals for pointers. If a pointer to `points` still exists on the stack at the time of the leak check (e.g., in a dead stack frame that hasn't been overwritten), LSan considers it \"still reachable\" and does NOT report it as a leak!\n\nTHIS is the key insight. LSan's leak detection scans the stack. If the `points` pointer value is still sitting in a stack slot (even in a dead/unused frame), LSan sees it as reachable and doesn't report.\n\nSo why would t4's points be \"definitely lost\" but t3's points be \"still reachable\"?\n\nConsider the stack layout at the moment main returns:\n\nt3: main is the ONLY frame. `points` is a local in main. When main returns... the frame is popped. The stack slot where `points` lived may still contain the pointer value, OR may be overwritten by subsequent calls (exit, atexit handlers, LSan's own code). Whether it's found depends on whether the value survives in a scanned region.\n\nActually LSan scans the CURRENT stack from current SP up to the top. After main returns and exit() is running, the stack has new frames from exit handling. The old main frame region (below current SP... no, above... stack grows down). Hmm, the region where main's locals lived is at HIGHER addresses (stack grows downward, so main's frame is at higher addresses than exit's frames). LSan scans from SP to the stack top — which INCLUDES main's old frame region. So if the `points` value is still there (not overwritten), it's \"still reachable\" → not reported.\n\nFor t4: there's a deeper call chain. `main` calls `get_points`. When get_points returns data, and main does `return status`, main's `points` local is on main's frame. Same story.\n\nHmm, this doesn't obviously differ between t3 and t4.\n\nWait — maybe I should reconsider. Maybe the difference is NOT the points leak in the error path, but something else. Let me re-read the user's messages.\n\nActually — the user in the previous message said \"все тесты под линуксом проходят даже с утечкой в 3 ей задаче\". And now asks why LSan was silent for t3.\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider: maybe the difference is which tests actually exercise the leak. \n\nt3 error tests: t3_c_not_digit_case, t3_c_too_few_args_case, t3_c_too_much_args_case, t3_c_extra_nan, t3_c_x_y_z_4k, t3_c_x_y_z_k4, t3_c_inf_coord, t3_c_x_ky_z, t3_c_x_yk_z — all of these hit the coordinate-error path where points leaks. These tests expect exit code 65 (DATA). And they PASS on Linux. So LSan did not report the leak (otherwise exit code would change).\n\nt4 error tests: t4_c_symbol_coords etc. — these hit the coordinate-error path where points leaks. t4_c_symbol_coords FAILED with \"expected 65, got 1\". So LSan reported it.\n\nSo the SAME type of leak, but different LSan behavior. Why?\n\nHmm, let me think about the exit code \"1\". Actually wait — maybe the \"1\" came from something entirely different than LSan. Let me reconsider run_case. Actually maybe LSan default exitcode on this system isn't 23.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Maybe there IS a real structural difference. Let me look at t4's get_points vs t3's main structure again.\n\nt4: `get_points` is a separate function that takes `struct Point** points`. It does `free(s)` and `return data;` WITHOUT freeing `*points`. main: `int status = get_points(...); if (status != 0) return status;`.\n\nt3: all in main. On error: `free(s); s = NULL; return data;`.\n\nStructurally, both leak points identically.\n\nOK here's another thought. Maybe the issue is about WHEN LSan runs and what's on the stack/registers at that point. LSan scans the stack of the thread. The `points` value might be in a REGISTER that's been clobbered, or in a stack slot.\n\nBut the key subtlety: LSan scans for pointers and considers blocks reachable if found. So whether a leak is reported depends on whether the pointer value happens to still be in a scanned memory region (stack slot, register, global). This is essentially a \"still reachable\" false-negative.\n\nSo the difference between t3 and t4 could simply be: in t3, the pointer to points happened to still be in a stack slot (or the compiler kept it in a register) at exit, so LSan considered it reachable. In t4, it got clobbered/overwritten, so it was \"definitely lost\".\n\nHmm, but that's fragile and not a satisfying \"logic\" answer. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the LSan design doc info: LSan detects leaks by scanning memory (stack, registers, globals, heap) for pointers. \"Still reachable\" blocks (a pointer exists somewhere) are NOT reported as leaks.\n\nSo for a leaked heap block to be REPORTED, NO pointer to it can be found anywhere in scanned memory.\n\nWhen does a pointer to `points` survive?\n- In a stack slot of a dead frame: survives if not overwritten.\n- In a register: registers get clobbered by subsequent calls.\n\nThe exit path after `return data` from main:\n1. main returns → CRT calls exit(data)\n2. exit runs atexit handlers (including LSan's leak check)\n3. During leak check, LSan scans the stack\n\nBetween main returning and LSan scanning, several function calls happen (exit, __run_exit_handlers, etc.), which use the stack ABOVE main's frame? No — stack grows DOWN. After main returns, SP is back at main's caller's SP (lower... no higher). Hmm, let me get this right: on x86-64, stack grows downward. main's frame is at lower addresses than the caller's frame. When main returns, SP moves UP to the caller's frame. New calls (exit etc.) create frames BELOW... no, at/above the caller's SP, growing downward again — but that region is BELOW the current SP, which is where exit's frames go. Main's old frame is BELOW... \n\nUgh, let me think clearly. Stack grows downward (toward lower addresses). `call main` pushes return address, main sets up frame. main's locals are at low addresses. When main returns, SP goes back up (higher addresses). Then `exit(65)` is called — its frames go at addresses BELOW the current SP (which is the high region). Wait no. \n\nActually: entry point (`_start`) calls `__libc_start_main` which calls `main`. When `main` returns, `__libc_start_main` calls `exit(main_ret)`. So the stack frame of `main` is at LOWER addresses than `__libc_start_main`'s frame. `exit` and its callees create frames at even LOWER addresses (growing down). So `main`'s frame (containing the `points` local) is ABOVE the frames of exit handlers, at higher addresses. LSan scans from current SP up to the top of the stack region (the stack top / max address). The top of the stack includes main's old frame. So the `points` value in main's old frame WOULD be scanned!\n\nSo if the `points` value is still in main's dead frame slot (not overwritten by LSan's own scanning, which happens in ITS frame below), it would be found → \"still reachable\" → NOT reported.\n\nHmm, so actually BOTH t3 and t4 should have \"still reachable\" points and NOT report leaks?! But t4 WAS reported. \n\nUnless — in t4, main's frame was different. Let me reconsider t4's structure: `get_points` is called from main. main's `points` local... when get_points returns, main does `return status`. The `points` pointer is in main's frame. It should survive like t3.\n\nThis is getting too speculative. Let me step back.\n\nActually — maybe the real difference is much simpler. Let me look at whether the t3 tests that PASS actually exercise the leak path at all, OR whether there's a difference in how LSan handles the exit code.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the exit code \"1\" more carefully. \n\nThe user's first message: \"ожидаемый код: 65, полученный: 1\". This was at t4_c_symbol_coords. \n\nWait — maybe exit code 1 came from the run_case itself or from a SHELL issue on the t4 path. Actually run_case runs the program via std::system. If the program prints the leak report and then... hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: LSan's `exitcode` default is 23 per the current docs. But maybe older GCC versions use 1? Or maybe the ASan `exitcode` (default 1) is used when LSan runs under ASan? Let me check: the docs say \"This can be different from the exit code used to signal ASan errors.\" So LSan uses its own exitcode=23. But the user saw 1.\n\nHmm, actually maybe the version of GCC on the user's Linux has LSan exitcode default = 1? Or maybe ASan's exitcode=1 takes precedence in some configs. Not critical.\n\nOK let me step back and think about what ACTUALLY differs between t3 and t4 that would make LSan silent for t3.\n\nLet me re-examine: maybe in t3, the leak is NOT reported because LSan considers `points` reachable due to the STACK containing a copy. But in t4... same.\n\nOR — maybe the real difference: in t3, the error test's stderr is checked with `--contains`, and the exit code is 65. If LSan reported a leak, exit code would become LSan's exitcode (23 or 1) and run_case would fail. But t3 tests PASS. So LSan did NOT report for t3.\n\nHmm wait, actually — let me reconsider whether t3 error tests were passing BEFORE the free(points) fix. The user says \"все тесты под линуксом проходят даже с утечкой в 3ей задаче\". So yes, t3 error tests passed on Linux with the leak present. And now asks WHY.\n\nSo I need to find a mechanism where LSan misses t3's leak but catches t4's.\n\nLet me look at the difference once more. Actually, let me look at the ACTUAL current t4 get_points code and t3 main code to spot ANY structural difference.\n\nt4 get_points error path:\n```c\nif (ex_code != 0) {\n    ...\n    free(s);\n    s = NULL;\n    return data;\n}\n```\nThis returns from get_points. *points not freed. \n\nt3 main error path:\n```c\nif (ex_code != 0) {\n    ...\n    free(s);\n    s = NULL;\n    return data;\n}\n```\nThis returns from main directly.\n\nBoth leak points identically.\n\nBUT WAIT. In t4, is there a SECOND leak location that's different? Let me look at t4's no_input path and realloc path:\n\nt4 get_points:\n```c\nif (*points_size == 0) {\n    fprintf(stderr, \"...\");\n    free(s);\n    free(*points);\n    return no_input;\n}\n```\nThis frees *points. OK.\n\nt3 main:\n```c\nif (points_size == 0) {\n    fprintf(stderr, \"...\");\n    free(s);\n    free(points);\n    return no_input;\n}\n```\nAlso frees.\n\nSo both free on no_input. The leak is ONLY the coordinate-error path. Identical.\n\nHmm. So structurally identical leak. The difference in LSan behavior must be environmental/stack-related.\n\nActually — WAIT. Maybe I should reconsider whether the t4 leak was actually reported at the coordinate-error test, or at a DIFFERENT test. The user's first error was t4_c_symbol_coords. But then later the user said \"task 4 c ... ругается что memory leak из объекта созданного на строке 128 - points\". Line 128 in t4 main.c (before/after edits) — let me check what's at line 128. Earlier read: line 128-129:\n```\n128:     int points_size = 0, points_capacity = 1;\n129:     struct Point* points = malloc(sizeof(struct Point));\n```\nWait, in the current file line 128 is `int points_size...` and line 129 is the malloc. But the user said \"строке 128 - points\". Close enough — the malloc is around there. So LSan reported the malloc'd points block leaking.\n\nNow, here's a thought: for t4, WHICH test triggered it? The user said the failing test was t4_c_symbol_coords (line 909). Input: \"2 wow 3\". This is a coordinate error (not a number). Program: reads radius \"1\" fine, then reads point \"2 wow 3\", parse_point fails → return data (65). points leaked. LSan reports → exit code 1 (or 23). run_case fails.\n\nFor t3: t3_c_not_digit_case input \"3 2 bam\". Same kind of test. Program reads \"3 2 bam\", parse fails, return data (65). points leaked. But LSan DIDN'T report.\n\nSo same leak, different LSan outcome. The mechanism must be about whether the leaked block is considered \"reachable\" at scan time.\n\nOK, I think the real explanation is about LSan's stack scanning and the \"still reachable\" classification. But WHY does t3 differ from t4?\n\nLet me think about the stack contents at exit more carefully for each.\n\nActually — here's a KEY difference I overlooked. In t4, `get_points` is a SEPARATE FUNCTION. The `points` variable in main is passed BY POINTER (`&points`) to get_points. Inside get_points, the parameter is `struct Point** points`. When get_points returns (error path), main's local `points` STILL holds the malloc'd address — but is it in a register or on the stack?\n\nActually, both are stored somewhere and both should survive similarly.\n\nHmm, let me consider ANOTHER possibility: maybe the difference is in the OPTIMIZATION level or... no, both built with same flags.\n\nWait, actually — let me reconsider the possibility that the t3 leak was classified as \"still reachable\" because the POINTER happened to survive, while in t4 the POINTER was in a register that got clobbered by the extra function call (get_points) machinery during exit. This is plausible: in t3, main directly returns data, `points` value sits in main's frame. In t4, main calls get_points (more code, more register pressure), and after get_points returns, the `points` value might have been moved around.\n\nBut honestly, this is all speculation about stack contents. The robust, defensible answer is:\n\n**LSan's leak classification depends on whether a pointer to the block is found during the scan of stack/registers/globals. Blocks with a surviving pointer are \"still reachable\" and NOT reported (default behavior). Whether the pointer survives is an artifact of code layout and the exact exit path — not a semantic difference in the code. So t3's leaked `points` happened to still have a pointer on the stack at exit → LSan stayed silent; t4's didn't → reported.**\n\nBut actually — hold on. Let me reconsider. There might be a much more concrete reason. Let me look at whether LSan on GCC reports \"still reachable\" or not by default. \n\nFrom LSan docs (the design doc / flags): LSan by default reports \"definitely lost\", \"indirectly lost\", \"possibly lost\", AND \"still reachable\" but... Actually, there's a well-known behavior: LSan by default does NOT fail on \"still reachable\" blocks. From the docs: \"If you want LSan to report still-reachable blocks, ...\" Actually let me recall the design doc. In the design doc, LSan distinguishes:\n- definitely lost: no pointers\n- indirectly lost: no pointers except through other leaked blocks\n- possibly lost: pointer points into the middle\n- still reachable: pointer found in a root (stack/global/register)\n\nAnd there's a flag `exitcode` — LSan calls _exit(exitcode) \"upon detecting leaks\". Does \"detecting leaks\" include still-reachable? I believe by default LSan treats only definitely/indirectly/possibly lost as errors, and still-reachable is just reported in the summary. Actually, I recall that by default LSan reports leaks and exits non-zero for the lost categories.\n\nHmm. So if t3's points was \"still reachable\", LSan would report it in the summary line (\"still reachable: 24 byte(s)\") but NOT call _exit. And run_case would see 65 → pass.\n\nIf t4's points was \"definitely lost\", LSan reports \"Direct leak\" and calls _exit → exit 1 (or 23) → run_case fails.\n\nThis fits! The mechanism: whether a stale pointer to the block survives on the stack at the moment of the leak scan.\n\nNow WHY does it differ? This is where I should be honest and not overclaim. But let me see if there's a structural reason.\n\nActually — you know what, let me reconsider. There IS potentially a structural difference: the NUMBER of stack frames and their lifetime.\n\nIn t3, `main` is the top frame. When main returns, the stack unwinds ONE frame, and exit() runs. Main's frame is just below the current SP region... and its content survives.\n\nIn t4, main calls get_points, which RETURNS before main returns. So get_points's frame is destroyed, then main returns. So main's frame is the same in both cases at exit.\n\nHmm, main's frame is what holds `points` in both. Should be equivalent.\n\nUnless the compiler optimized `points` into a register in one case. With -O0 (debug), everything is on the stack. So both t3 and t4 with -O0 would have points on the stack. Both should survive...\n\nBut wait — the leak test output: t4 reported a leak. So in t4, the pointer did NOT survive on the stack at scan time. How?\n\nOH. Maybe the issue is that LSan's leak check runs and during its OWN scan it walks the stack, and the \"still reachable\" pointer on the stack from main's dead frame — is that region actually scanned? LSan needs to know the stack bounds. It uses the current thread's stack top (from the thread descriptor / pthread attrs) and the current SP. The dead frames of main are within [SP, stack_top]? \n\nStack grows down. Thread stack top = highest address. main's frame is near the top (main is called early). When main returns and exit() runs, the new frames (exit, atexit handlers, LSan check) are created at addresses BELOW main's frame (stack grows down, so newer frames are at lower addresses than main's frame). So main's frame is at HIGHER addresses than current SP. LSan scans from current SP UP to stack_top → includes main's frame region → finds the pointer.\n\nSo in BOTH t3 and t4, if main's frame still holds the `points` value, LSan should find it → still reachable → silent.\n\nBut t4 was NOT silent. So in t4, main's frame did NOT hold a pointer to points at scan time. Why?\n\nHmm — maybe in t4, between get_points returning and the leak scan, main's frame gets overwritten? No, main's frame is untouched after main returns.\n\nWait, actually — maybe the pointer in t4 was NOT in main's frame. Let me reconsider: In t4, `points` is declared in main. But `get_points` receives `&points`. Inside get_points, it uses `*points` to access. When get_points returns, the value of `points` (in main) is unchanged. So main's frame holds it.\n\nUnless the compiler kept `points` in a register across the get_points call (callee-saved register like %rbx). Then it's in a register, not on the stack. At exit, registers are clobbered by exit machinery → pointer lost → definitely lost → reported.\n\nAnd in t3, maybe `points` stayed on the stack (not in a register) because main uses it directly in a loop.\n\nBut this is all -O0... with -O0 gcc keeps locals on the stack. Hmm.\n\nHonestly, I don't have enough info to give a definitive \"why t3 vs t4\" answer with high confidence. The honest, defensible answer is the LSan reachability mechanism, with the caveat that the exact classification depends on stack/register state at exit, which is an artifact of the specific binaries — NOT a difference in the leak itself.\n\nBUT — let me reconsider once more. There's actually a MUCH more concrete possibility I haven't fully considered: maybe in t3, the leak was NEVER EXERCISED in the tests that passed! Let me check: did the t3 error tests exist at the time the user ran on Linux? The user said \"все тесты под линуксом проходят даже с утечкой в 3ей задаче\". The new t3 tests (too_much, extra_nan, etc.) were added in commit 419221e. Was that before or after the Linux run?\n\nThe commit history shows:\n- 419221e added new tests for t3-t4 (Aug 30)\n- 25de5d4 add free(points) in t4 (12:38)\n- 3c559d5 removed excessive memory free (13:22)\n- 713cb6a added free(points) in t3 (13:42)\n\nSo the new t3 tests were added BEFORE the t3 free fix. And the user's Linux runs (with the t4 failure at symbol_coords) — symbol_coords is a t4 test. The user hit t4_c_symbol_coords first. But before reaching t4 in `make test`, all t3 tests must pass. So t3 error tests DID run on Linux and DID pass (with the leak!).\n\nSo t3's leak was exercised (t3_c_not_digit_case, t3_c_too_few_args_case, etc. all hit the coordinate-error path, returning 65) and LSan stayed silent. Meanwhile t4's identical leak was reported.\n\nSo it IS a real asymmetry. The mechanism must be the \"still reachable\" classification.\n\nLet me reconsider the exit code detail: run_case saw \"1\" not \"23\". If LSan called _exit(23), run_case would see 23. So maybe the exit code 1 came from something ELSE in t4. OR t